<h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Choose the correct option for each of the following questions.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> A block of mass m<sub>1</sub> = 2 kg rests on top of a block of mass m<sub>2</sub> = 4 kg, which is placed on a smooth horizontal floor. The coefficient of static friction between the two blocks is &mu;<sub>s</sub> = 0.4. What is the maximum horizontal force F that can be applied to the lower block m<sub>2</sub> such that both blocks move together without slipping? (Take g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup>) </div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 8 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 16 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 24 N</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 12 N</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> A smooth wedge of mass M with an inclined face making an angle &alpha; with the horizontal is placed on a frictionless horizontal floor. A block of mass m is allowed to slide down the inclined face of the wedge. The horizontal acceleration of the wedge is: </div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m g sin&alpha; cos&alpha;</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">M + m sin<sup>2</sup>&alpha;</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m g sin&alpha;</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">M + m</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">M g sin&alpha; cos&alpha;</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">M + m cos<sup>2</sup>&alpha;</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m g cos&alpha;</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">M + m sin&alpha;</span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> A time-dependent force <b>F&#8407;</b> = (6t<sup>2</sup> i&#x0302; + 4t j&#x0302;) N acts on a particle of mass 2 kg initially at rest at the origin. What is the speed of the particle at t = 3 s? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 27 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 15 m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 9&radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">5</span> m/s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 3&radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;">10</span> m/s</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A particle moves in a circle of radius R with a constant tangential acceleration a<sub>t</sub>. If the net acceleration vector makes an angle of 45^&deg; with the velocity vector at time t, then t is equal to: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;"><span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">R</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">a<sub>t</sub></span></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">R</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">a<sub>t</sub></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &radic;<span style="border-top:1px solid #000;padding-top:1px;margin-left:1px;"><span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2R</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">a<sub>t</sub></span></span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">2R</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">a<sub>t</sub></span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> A uniform rope of mass M and length L is pulled along a frictionless horizontal surface by a constant horizontal force F applied at one end. What is the tension in the rope at a distance x from the end where the force is applied? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) F &le;ft(1 - <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">x</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">L</span></span>\right)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) F <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">x</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">L</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) F &le;ft(1 + <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">x</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">L</span></span>\right)</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) F</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q6.</strong> A rocket of mass 1000 kg burns fuel at the rate of 10 kg/s with an exhaust velocity of 500 m/s relative to the rocket. If the rocket is fired vertically upwards from the ground, the initial net acceleration of the rocket is (take g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup>): </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 5 m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) -5 m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 0 m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 10 m/s<sup>2</sup></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q7.</strong> What is the minimum force required to pull a body of mass m along a rough horizontal surface having angle of friction &phi;? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) m g sin&phi;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) m g cos&phi;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) m g tan&phi;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) &mu; m g</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q8.</strong> A bead of mass m is free to slide on a smooth circular wire loop of radius R placed in a vertical plane. The wire loop rotates about its vertical diameter with a constant angular velocity &omega;. The angle &theta; made by the radius vector joining the bead to the center with the downward vertical in equilibrium (for &omega;<sup>2</sup> > g/R) is given by: </div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) cos&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">g</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&omega;<sup>2</sup> R</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) sin&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">g</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&omega;<sup>2</sup> R</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) tan&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&omega;<sup>2</sup> R</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">g</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) cos&theta; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&omega;<sup>2</sup> R</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">g</span></span></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q9.</strong> Two masses m<sub>1</sub> = 3 kg and m<sub>2</sub> = 5 kg are connected by a light string passing over a frictionless fixed pulley. The system is released from rest. What is the downward acceleration of mass m<sub>2</sub>? (Take g = 10 m/s<sup>2</sup>) </div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 2.5 m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 3.75 m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 5.0 m/s<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 1.25 m/s<sup>2</sup></li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q10.</strong> A body of mass m slides down a rough inclined plane of inclination &theta; with a constant velocity. The coefficient of kinetic friction &mu;<sub>k</sub> between the body and the plane is equal to: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) sin&theta;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) cos&theta;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) tan&theta;</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) cot&theta;</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q11.</strong> An elevator cab of mass m accelerates upwards with an acceleration a = g/2. A passenger of mass m<sub>0</sub> inside the elevator experiences an apparent weight of: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) m<sub>0</sub> g / 2</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) m<sub>0</sub> g</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 3 m<sub>0</sub> g / 2</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 2 m<sub>0</sub> g</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q12.</strong> A particle of mass m is kept on a smooth sphere of radius R at its topmost point. The sphere is fixed. If the particle is given a slight push, at what height h from the top point will it lose contact with the sphere? </div><div style='text-align:center;margin:8px 0;'><img src='data:image/png;base64,' style='max-width:320px;max-height:220px;' alt='Diagram'></div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) R/3</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 2R/3</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) R/2</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) R/4</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q13.</strong> A force-time graph for a particle moving along a straight line shows a triangular area with base &Delta; t = 4 s and maximum height F<sub>\max</sub> = 10 N. The total impulse imparted to the particle is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 40 N s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 20 N s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 10 N s</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 80 N s</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q14.</strong> A block of mass m is held against a vertical wall by applying a horizontal force F. If the coefficient of static friction is &mu;, the minimum force F required to prevent the block from falling is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &mu; m g</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m g</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&mu;</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">&mu;</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">m g</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) m g</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q15.</strong> In a non-inertial frame of reference accelerating horizontally with acceleration a<sub>0</sub>, the net pseudo force acting on a particle of mass m is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) m a<sub>0</sub> in the direction of a<sub>0</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) m a<sub>0</sub> opposite to the direction of a<sub>0</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Zero</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) m g vertically downwards</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Fill in the missing values or terms in each of the following statements.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> The minimum horizontal force required to push a block of mass m against a vertical wall to keep it from sliding down, given coefficient of friction &mu;, is _______. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The magnitude of thrust on a rocket burning fuel at a rate r = -<span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">dm</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">dt</span></span> with exhaust speed u<sub>r</sub> relative to the rocket engine is equal to _______. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> A block resting on a rough inclined plane of inclination &theta; begins to slide down when tan&theta; just exceeds the coefficient of _______ friction. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> For a vehicle taking a circular turn of radius R on a smooth banked road inclined at angle &theta;, the optimum velocity without relying on friction is _______. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> When a spring balance carrying a body of mass m drops freely under gravity, the reading on the spring balance is _______. </div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section C: True / False</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: State whether the following statements are True or False.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Static friction is a self-adjusting force whose value ranges from zero up to a maximum limiting friction value f<sub>s</sub><sup>\max</sup> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> N. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Action and reaction forces according to Newton's Third Law act on the same body and cancel each other out. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Pseudo forces must be introduced to apply Newton's second law in non-inertial (accelerating) reference frames. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> Centripetal force does non-zero work on a body undergoing uniform circular motion. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> The coefficient of kinetic friction &mu;<sub>k</sub> is generally slightly less than the coefficient of static friction &mu;<sub>s</sub> for the same pair of surfaces. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section D: Very Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer the following questions in brief.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> State the Impulse-Momentum Theorem mathematically and express its S.I. units. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Why is static friction called a self-adjusting force? </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> A helicopter of mass M accelerates upwards with an acceleration a. Write down the force equation for the vertical lift force F<sub>L</sub> exerted by the surrounding air on the helicopter rotors. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> Explain why a cricketer pulls his hands backward while catching a fast-moving cricket ball. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Write the constraint relationship between the accelerations a<sub>1</sub> and a<sub>2</sub> of two blocks connected by an inextensible string passing over a single movable pulley connected to one of the blocks. </div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section E: Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer all questions with appropriate derivation and explanations where necessary.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> A block of mass m<sub>1</sub> = 5 kg rests on a rough horizontal surface with coefficient of static friction &mu;<sub>s</sub> = 0.5. A second block of mass m<sub>2</sub> = 2 kg is placed on top of m<sub>1</sub> with friction coefficient &mu;<sub>12</sub> = 0.3 between them. Calculate the maximum horizontal force F that can be applied to mass m<sub>1</sub> such that mass m<sub>2</sub> does not slip on m<sub>1</sub>. </div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: NCERT Diagram: ]</div>
<div style='margin-top:30px;text-align:center;font-size:9pt;font-style:italic;color:#666;border-top:1px solid #ccc;padding-top:8px;'>
